{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.5,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U7", "U11", "S2"],
      "pins": ["U7.44", "U7.47", "U11.6", "S2.1"],
      "nets": ["NET0040", "NET0015"],
      "obs": "On SkylabModule (sheet /SkylabModule/, page 1/4) the net on U7 pin 44, whose symbol function text reads 'HW_RESET', is NET0040 — the same global label that on U11 (BBQ10KBD) pin 6 is ROW1. The physical reset slide switch S2 (sheet /EthernetAndPower/, page 4/4) pin 1 is wired to NET0015, which lands on U7 pin 47 (symbol text 'I2C_CLK'), not on the HW_RESET pin.",
      "exp": "The specification states S1 is wired as the HW_RESET switch: 'S1 ... pin1→GND, pin2→HW_RESET (SKW92A pin 45)'. The module's HW_RESET input must be a dedicated net tied to the switch, and must not be shared with a keyboard-matrix row.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "SkylabModule page 1/4: U7 pin 44 'HW_RESET' → NET0040; U11 pin 6 'ROW1' → NET0040; U7 pin 47 'I2C_CLK' → NET0015; EthernetAndPower page 4/4: S2 pin 1 → NET0015"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "S1 ... is, per the netlist, wired as a reset button (S1.1→GND, S1.2→HW_RESET→SKW92A pin 45 HW_RESET)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The module's hardware-reset pin is driven by the keyboard scan matrix, so scanning ROW1 will glitch or hold the SoC in reset (board may fail to boot); meanwhile the slide switch toggles an I2C clock line and cannot reset the device.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U7", "Q3", "U11", "R1"],
      "pins": ["U7.31", "Q3.1", "U11.23", "R1.2"],
      "nets": ["NET0022"],
      "obs": "On SkylabModule page 1/4 the gate of the P-channel display-power switch Q3 (NTR2101P) is pulled up to +3V3 by R1 (10 k) and tied to U7 pin 31; the net at that node is NET0022, which is also the label on U11 pin 23 (LEDK_2_3, a keyboard backlight LED cathode).",
      "exp": "The specification assigns SKW92A pin 31 exclusively as the GPIO that drives the high-side EPD power switch ('U5.31 ... repurposed here as a general output' for Q2's gate). A high-side power-switch gate must not share a net with a matrix-scanned LED line.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "SkylabModule page 1/4: R1 10k between +3V3 and Q3 pin 1; Q3 pin 1 / U7 pin 31 / U11 pin 23 (LEDK_2_3) all carry NET0022"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "U5.31 (SKW92A GPIO ... repurposed here as a general output). Q2.2 (source) ties to +3V3; Q2.3 (drain) ties to the Display_Power_Switch net"}
      ],
      "impact": "Operating the keyboard backlight toggles the e-paper display's power rail (and the 10 k gate pull-up fights the LED driver), causing unintended display power cycling and unreliable backlight control.",
      "confidence": 0.45
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The build uses a systematically renumbered reference set but appears to mis-map several SKW92A GPIOs: the HW_RESET pin is bridged to a keyboard-matrix row (and the reset switch is on an I2C net), and the display-power MOSFET gate is bridged to a keyboard backlight LED line — both contradicting the specification's stated connectivity."
}

